You are on the Backend track. Pick one project idea below and implement it in 2 weeks. Keep the domain small; the goal is to touch every required skill, not to build something complex.

Tech stack
Node.js + TypeScript + Express

Supabase for the database (Postgres) and file storage bucket

Supabase can run locally via the Supabase CLI (supabase start) — no cloud account required to develop

Use Supabase only for the database and the storage bucket — not for auth

Custom authentication — you implement register/login yourself with password hashing (bcrypt/argon2) + JWT. This is a required learning objective; do not use Supabase Auth or any drop-in auth provider.

Required skills
 User registration + authentication (sign up, log in, protected routes)

 CRUD over one main entity (create, read, list, update, delete)

 Persistence in the database (Postgres via Supabase)

 Files + storage buckets (upload a file, store it in a bucket, save/return its URL)

Whichever idea you pick, every list/read endpoint must return only the current user's data. Authorization is part of the exercise.


Idea 3 — Bookshelf API
A user tracks the books they own or want to read, each with a cover image.

Main CRUD entity: Book (title, author, status = want/reading/done, rating, cover URL)

Auth: register/login; each shelf is private

Persistence: books table linked to the user id

Files/bucket: upload a cover image → book-covers bucket → save URL on the book

Sample endpoints



POST   /auth/register
POST   /auth/login
GET    /books              # list / filter by status
POST   /books
PATCH  /books/:id          # e.g. change status or rating
DELETE /books/:id
POST   /books/:id/cover



Definition of Done (applies to any idea)
 Register + login work; passwords are never stored in plain text

 Protected routes reject requests without a valid token (401)

 Full CRUD on the main entity, scoped to the authenticated user

 Data persists in Supabase Postgres (survives a server restart)

 File upload works end-to-end: file lands in a Supabase Storage bucket, its URL is saved on the entity, and reading the entity returns a usable URL

 Input validation + sensible error responses (400 / 401 / 404)

 README.md with setup steps (.env variables, how to run Supabase locally, how to start the API)

 Basic tests for at least the auth flow and one CRUD path

 Runs locally with a single documented command

Architecture & Clean Code guidelines
The goal isn't a perfect architecture, it's separating concerns so the code stays readable and testable. Aim for a layered structure.

Layered structure (thin routes → services → data access)
The structure bellow is a suggestion; the goal is to separate the concerns and keep things simple.



src/
  config/        # env loading, supabase client creation (one place)
  routes/        # HTTP wiring only: path + middleware + call controller
  controllers/   # parse request, call a service, shape the response
  services/      # business logic — the "what happens" (no req/res here)
  repositories/  # data access — talks to Supabase/DB, returns plain objects
  middleware/    # auth (verify JWT), error handler, validation
  schemas/       # Zod schemas / DTO types for input validation
  utils/         # small shared helpers
  types/         # shared TypeScript types
  app.ts         # build the Express app
  server.ts      # start the server
Principles to apply (SOLID / clean code, lightly)
Single Responsibility: a route wires HTTP, a service holds logic, a repository does data access. Don't put DB queries in a route or business rules in a controller.

Thin controllers, fat-free services: controllers only translate HTTP ↔ service calls. Business rules live in services and know nothing about req/res.

Dependency inversion (lightweight): pass the Supabase client into repositories/services instead of importing a global. Makes testing and swapping easy.

Validate at the boundary: parse and validate every request body/params with Zod in middleware, so the rest of the code trusts its inputs.

One way to handle errors: throw typed errors from services; a single error-handling middleware turns them into HTTP responses. No try/catch scattered in every route.

Meaningful names: getRecipesForUser, not handle2. Functions do one thing; keep them short (aim < ~20 lines).

DRY, but not prematurely: extract a helper the second time you copy code, not the first.

No magic values: status codes, bucket names, limits → named constants / config.

Config over hardcoding: URLs, keys, ports come from env (config/), never inline.

Keep functions pure where you can: easier to reason about and to test.

Watch out for (common intern smells)
Business logic inside route handlers → move it to a service.

The Supabase client imported and instantiated in five files → create it once in config/.

Returning raw DB rows with sensitive fields → map to a response shape (DTO).

Deeply nested if/try pyramids → use early returns and the error middleware.

Cyclomatic complexity creeping up on a function (many branches) → split it.

Security: storing users & authenticating them
You implement authentication yourself — this is a core learning objective. Handling credentials safely is required, not a stretch goal. Supabase is only your database and file store here; the users table and the login logic are your own code.

Storing users
Never store plaintext passwords. Hash with bcrypt (cost ≥ 10) or argon2. The salt is generated and embedded by the algorithm — store only the resulting hash string.

Store the hash in a password_hash column on your own users table. There is no "decrypt" — on login you hash the attempt and compare against the stored hash.

Never log passwords or full tokens, and never return password_hash in any API response (map to a safe user DTO).

Least data: store only what you need (email + hash + profile basics). Don't collect PII you won't use.

Authenticating (register / login / protected routes)
Register: validate input → hash the password → insert the user → return a safe user object (no hash).

Login: look up by email → compare with bcrypt.compare → on success issue a JWT.

JWT: sign with a strong secret from env (JWT_SECRET), set a sensible expiry (e.g. 15–60 min; add refresh tokens as a stretch). Verify the token in auth middleware on every protected route and derive the user id from the verified token, never from the request body.

Consistent auth errors: return a generic 401 invalid credentials — don't reveal whether the email exists.

Rate-limit login/register routes to slow brute-force attempts.

Applies throughout
Secrets in env: DB URL, Supabase keys, JWT_SECRET come from .env (with a committed .env.example), never hardcoded or logged.

Transport: assume HTTPS in production; never send credentials in query strings or URLs.

Row ownership: every query for the main entity is scoped to the authenticated user's id from the token.

Be ready to explain in the demo: why is a password hash not reversible, where exactly does the password live in your system, and what does the JWT prove on each request?

Suggested 2-week milestones
Days 1–2: Project setup (Express + TS), Supabase local running, DB schema for the entity

Days 3–5: Auth (register/login, JWT middleware, protected routes)

Days 6–8: Full CRUD on the main entity, scoped to the user

Days 9–10: File upload to the storage bucket + URL persistence

Days 11–12: Validation, error handling, tests

Days 13–14: README, cleanup, demo prep

Stretch goals (if you finish early)
Pick a few — ordered roughly easy → hard. Don't do all of them; go deep on 2–3.

API quality

 Pagination + filtering + sorting on the list endpoint (?page=&limit=&sort=)

 A search endpoint over the entity

 Request validation with a schema library (Zod) + a shared error-handling middleware

 Consistent error response shape ({ error: { code, message } })

 Rate limiting on auth routes (express-rate-limit)

Auth & security

 Refresh tokens + logout (token invalidation)

 Password reset flow

 Role-based access (e.g. user vs admin) on at least one route

 Awareness pass: how does your code prevent SQL injection / leaking other users' data?

Files

 Validate upload type + size; reject anything unexpected

 Generate and store a thumbnail for image uploads

 Use signed URLs with expiry instead of public URLs

Data model

 Add a second, related entity (one-to-many) with its own endpoints

 Database migrations checked into the repo; add useful indexes

 Soft delete (deleted_at) instead of hard delete

Engineering

 Dockerize the API + docker-compose (API + local Supabase)

 CI with GitHub Actions (lint + tests on every push)

 Deploy to a free host (e.g. Vercel) with a live URL

 Raise test coverage: integration tests for every CRUD path

 Auto-generated API docs (OpenAPI / Swagger UI)

